"""Expanded content for the procedure pages (English), added 17 Oct 2026.
The home page keeps its short summaries; these fields only appear on /procedures/<slug>/.
Keys per slug: desc (meta description), intro (paragraphs), prep (before), day (on the day), after, risks (replaces the short one),
alarm (get help straight away), alts (other tests and options), faqs (added after the existing two).
Medical content is conservative and general: no medicine doses or brand names, no third-space endoscopy (POEM/ESD)."""

MORE = {
"upper-gi-endoscopy": dict(
 desc="Upper GI endoscopy in Gurugram: why it is done, how to prepare, what happens on the day, recovery, risks and common questions, explained by gastroenterologist Dr. Kshitij Lochab.",
 intro=["An upper GI endoscopy (also called a gastroscopy or OGD) lets the doctor look directly at the lining of the food pipe (oesophagus), stomach and the first part of the small intestine (duodenum). A thin, flexible tube with a light and camera is passed through the mouth while you lie on your side.",
        "It is the most useful test for persistent acidity, upper belly pain, swallowing problems and bleeding, because it shows the problem directly and allows small tissue samples (biopsies) to be taken painlessly. Many problems can also be treated during the same test, such as stopping a bleeding ulcer or widening a narrowing."],
 prep=["Nothing to eat for 6 to 8 hours before. Small sips of plain water are usually allowed until 2 hours before, unless you are told otherwise.",
       "Tell the doctor if you take blood thinners, diabetes medicines or insulin, as these may need a change for the day.",
       "Bring your old reports, prescriptions and any previous endoscopy reports.",
       "If you will have sedation, come with an adult who can take you home.",
       "Remove dentures and spectacles before the test."],
 day="A local anaesthetic spray numbs the throat, and you may be given light sedation through a vein. You lie on your left side with a small mouth guard to protect your teeth. The scope is passed gently; you can breathe normally throughout. A little air is used to open up the stomach, which can make you feel full or burp. The test usually takes 5 to 10 minutes, and biopsies are painless.",
 after=["You rest for 30 to 60 minutes if you had sedation. Do not drive, operate machinery or sign important documents for the rest of the day.",
        "Eat and drink once the throat numbness wears off, usually after about an hour. Start with something light.",
        "A mild sore throat or bloating for a few hours is common.",
        "The doctor explains the findings the same day. Biopsy reports usually take 5 to 7 days."],
 risks="Upper GI endoscopy is a very safe, routine test. A mild sore throat for a day is common. A reaction to the sedative, bleeding after a biopsy, or a tear in the food pipe or stomach are rare, and the risk is slightly higher when treatment such as dilatation is done during the test.",
 alarm=["Severe chest, neck or belly pain after the test","Vomiting blood or black, tarry stools","Fever, breathlessness or difficulty swallowing"],
 alts="A barium swallow or CT scan can show some problems, but they cannot take biopsies or give treatment. For most symptoms of the food pipe and stomach, endoscopy is the most accurate test.",
 faqs=[["How long does an endoscopy take?","The test itself takes about 5 to 10 minutes. With preparation and recovery, plan for 1 to 2 hours at the hospital."],
       ["Do I need to stop my blood thinner?","Do not stop it on your own. Some blood thinners can continue for a simple endoscopy; others are paused if biopsies or treatment are likely. Your doctor will plan this with the doctor who prescribed them."],
       ["Can I go home the same day?","Yes. Routine endoscopy is a day procedure and you go home within a couple of hours."],
       ["Where can I get an endoscopy in Gurugram?","Dr. Kshitij Lochab sees patients at Arcura Clinic, Sector 51, Apollo Clinic, Sector 52, and Apollo Hospitals, Golf Course Road. The endoscopy is done in a hospital endoscopy unit. Call or WhatsApp to book a consultation first."]]),

"colonoscopy": dict(
 desc="Colonoscopy in Gurugram: why it is done, bowel preparation, what to expect, polyp removal, recovery, risks and screening advice, explained by gastroenterologist Dr. Kshitij Lochab.",
 intro=["A colonoscopy examines the whole large bowel (colon) and the end of the small intestine with a thin, flexible camera passed through the back passage. It is done under sedation, so most people feel little or nothing.",
        "It is the most accurate test for blood in the stool, a lasting change in bowel habit, unexplained anaemia and inflammatory bowel disease. Its biggest advantage is that polyps, which can slowly turn into cancer, can be found and removed during the same test."],
 prep=["Eat a low-fibre diet for 1 to 2 days before: avoid seeds, salads, whole grains, dal with skins and fruits with peel.",
       "The day before, switch to clear liquids such as water, clear soup, coconut water and light-coloured juices without pulp.",
       "Take the bowel cleansing drink exactly as instructed, usually split between the evening before and the morning of the test. Your stools should become clear, yellow liquid.",
       "Stop iron tablets about a week before, as they leave dark residue in the bowel.",
       "Tell the doctor about blood thinners, diabetes medicines and insulin, which may need changes.",
       "Come with an adult who can take you home after sedation."],
 day="You lie on your left side and are given sedation through a vein. The scope is passed gently around the colon while air or carbon dioxide opens it up for a clear view. Polyps are removed and biopsies taken as needed, which is painless. The test usually takes 20 to 40 minutes.",
 after=["You rest for about an hour. Bloating and passing wind for a few hours are normal.",
        "Eat normally afterwards unless you are given special advice after polyp removal.",
        "Do not drive or make important decisions for the rest of the day.",
        "A streak of blood after polyp removal can occur. Biopsy and polyp reports take about a week."],
 risks="Colonoscopy is safe for most people. Bloating and cramps for a few hours are common. Bleeding after polyp removal happens in a small number of people, and a tear in the bowel wall is rare, more so after removing large polyps. Reactions to sedation are uncommon.",
 alarm=["Severe or worsening belly pain, or a hard, swollen belly","More than a few spoonfuls of blood from the back passage","Fever, dizziness or fainting"],
 alts="Stool tests for hidden blood and CT colonography can help in some situations, but neither can take biopsies or remove polyps. If they are abnormal, a colonoscopy is still needed.",
 faqs=[["How long does a colonoscopy take?","Usually 20 to 40 minutes, plus time for preparation and recovery. Plan for about 2 to 3 hours at the hospital."],
       ["When should I have a screening colonoscopy?","Many guidelines suggest starting around age 45 to 50 for people at average risk. If a parent, brother or sister had colon cancer or large polyps, screening usually starts earlier. Discuss your own risk with your doctor."],
       ["What if my bowel preparation is not clear?","Small polyps can be missed, and the test may need to be repeated. Following the diet and drink timing carefully makes a big difference."],
       ["Where can I get a colonoscopy in Gurugram?","Dr. Kshitij Lochab sees patients at Arcura Clinic, Sector 51, Apollo Clinic, Sector 52, and Apollo Hospitals, Golf Course Road. The colonoscopy is done in a hospital endoscopy unit after a consultation."]]),

"endoscopic-ultrasound-eus": dict(
 desc="Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) in Gurugram for pancreas, bile duct and stomach problems: uses, EUS biopsy (FNA/FNB), drainage, preparation, recovery and risks. Dr. Kshitij Lochab, Advanced Fellowship in EUS and ERCP.",
 intro=["Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) combines endoscopy with a tiny ultrasound probe on the tip of the scope. Because the probe sits right next to the organs being examined, it gives sharper, closer pictures than an ultrasound done over the skin. It is especially good for the pancreas, bile duct, gallbladder, the walls of the food pipe and stomach, and nearby lymph nodes.",
        "EUS can find very small stones in the bile duct, small pancreatic lumps and cysts that CT or MRI scans may miss or leave uncertain. A thin needle can be passed through the scope to take samples (EUS-guided FNA or FNB) without any cut on the skin.",
        "EUS is also used for treatment: draining fluid collections after pancreatitis, draining a blocked bile duct when ERCP is not possible, and nerve blocks to ease pain from the pancreas. A lower EUS through the back passage is used for some rectal and anal problems.",
        "Dr. Kshitij Lochab has an Advanced Fellowship in EUS and ERCP and performs both diagnostic and therapeutic EUS."],
 prep=["Nothing to eat for 6 to 8 hours before.",
       "Bring all CT, MRI and ultrasound reports and images, and previous endoscopy reports.",
       "Blood tests, including clotting and platelet counts, may be needed if a biopsy or drainage is planned.",
       "Tell the doctor about blood thinners, diabetes medicines and allergies. Blood thinners may need to be paused if a biopsy is likely.",
       "Some people are given an antibiotic, for example when a cyst is sampled.",
       "Come with an adult who can take you home."],
 day="EUS is usually done under deeper sedation, so you are asleep and comfortable. You lie on your left side, and the scope is passed through the mouth into the stomach and duodenum. A simple EUS takes about 20 to 30 minutes; with biopsies or drainage it can take up to an hour. Needle samples are taken through the wall of the stomach or duodenum, so there is no skin wound.",
 after=["You are watched for 1 to 2 hours. A sore throat or bloating for a day is common.",
        "Most people go home the same day. After drainage of a collection or placement of a stent, an overnight stay may be advised.",
        "Eat light food once you are fully awake, unless told otherwise.",
        "Biopsy reports usually take 3 to 7 days. The doctor explains the next steps, which may include further tests or treatment."],
 risks="Diagnostic EUS has a very low risk, similar to a routine endoscopy. When a needle sample is taken, there is a small risk of bleeding, infection or pancreatitis, roughly in 1 or 2 out of every 100 people for pancreatic samples. Therapeutic procedures such as drainage carry higher risks, which are explained in detail before the procedure.",
 alarm=["Severe belly pain, especially spreading to the back","Fever or chills","Vomiting blood, black stools, or chest pain"],
 alts="CT, MRI and MRCP scans are often done first and are complementary to EUS. EUS is chosen when scans are unclear, when a small lesion needs a closer look, or when a tissue sample or drainage is needed without surgery.",
 faqs=[["Is EUS painful?","No. It is done under sedation, and most people remember nothing of the procedure. A mild sore throat afterwards is common."],
       ["Is EUS better than CT or MRI for the pancreas?","For small lumps, small cysts and tiny bile duct stones, EUS often shows more detail, and it can take samples. CT and MRI give a wider view of the whole abdomen. The tests are often used together."],
       ["Who needs EUS for a pancreatic cyst?","Many cysts are small and harmless. EUS is advised when a cyst has worrying features on a scan, is growing, or when its type is unclear and a fluid sample would change the plan."],
       ["Where can I get EUS in Gurugram (Gurgaon)?","Dr. Kshitij Lochab, who has an Advanced Fellowship in EUS and ERCP, sees patients at Arcura Clinic, Sector 51, Apollo Clinic, Sector 52, and Apollo Hospitals, Golf Course Road. EUS is done in a hospital endoscopy unit after a consultation and review of your scans."]]),

"ercp": dict(
 desc="ERCP in Gurugram for bile duct stones, jaundice and blocked bile ducts: how it works, preparation, stenting, recovery and risks. Dr. Kshitij Lochab, Advanced Fellowship in EUS and ERCP.",
 intro=["ERCP (endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography) treats problems of the bile duct and pancreatic duct without open surgery. A side-viewing scope is passed through the mouth to the opening of the bile duct in the duodenum. A thin tube is passed into the duct, dye is injected and X-ray pictures show stones or narrowings.",
        "Through the scope, the opening can be widened with a small cut (sphincterotomy), stones can be pulled out with a balloon or basket, and plastic or metal stents can be placed to drain a blocked duct. This quickly relieves jaundice, itching and infection of the bile duct (cholangitis).",
        "Because ERCP is a treatment procedure, scans such as MRCP or EUS are usually done first to confirm that it is needed. Dr. Kshitij Lochab has an Advanced Fellowship in EUS and ERCP."],
 prep=["Nothing to eat for 6 to 8 hours before.",
       "Blood tests, including liver tests and clotting, are checked beforehand.",
       "Tell the doctor about blood thinners, diabetes medicines, allergies (including to X-ray dye or iodine), and if you may be pregnant.",
       "ERCP is usually done as a day-care or overnight hospital stay. A family member should come with you.",
       "The benefits and risks are explained, and you are asked to sign a consent form."],
 day="You lie on your tummy or side and are given deep sedation or anaesthesia. The procedure uses X-rays, so staff wear lead aprons. It usually takes 30 to 90 minutes, depending on what needs to be done, such as removing several stones or placing a stent.",
 after=["You are watched for several hours, and many people stay overnight.",
        "Nothing is eaten for a few hours; then clear fluids are started, followed by food if you feel well.",
        "Mild throat soreness and bloating are common.",
        "If a plastic stent was placed, a repeat ERCP to change or remove it is usually planned within about 3 months. Do not miss this date.",
        "If stones came from the gallbladder, removal of the gallbladder is often advised to stop them coming back."],
 risks="ERCP is more complex than a routine endoscopy. Pancreatitis is the most common complication, occurring in a few out of every 100 people; it is usually mild but can be serious. Bleeding after sphincterotomy, infection and a tear in the duodenum are uncommon. Steps are taken to lower these risks, and they are explained before the procedure.",
 alarm=["Severe belly pain, especially going through to the back","Fever with shivering, or worsening yellow eyes","Vomiting blood or black stools"],
 alts="MRCP (a special MRI) and EUS can diagnose bile duct stones and narrowings but cannot treat them. If ERCP is not possible, a bile duct can be drained through the skin by a radiologist (PTBD) or through EUS, and some problems are treated with surgery.",
 faqs=[["Is ERCP the same as MRCP?","No. MRCP is a scan that only takes pictures. ERCP is a procedure that can remove stones and place stents, so it is used when treatment is needed."],
       ["How long will I stay in hospital?","Often one night, sometimes only a few hours. Longer stays are needed if there is infection, pancreatitis or another illness."],
       ["Can bile duct stones come back?","They can, especially if the gallbladder still has stones. Removing the gallbladder after ERCP greatly lowers this risk."],
       ["Where can I get ERCP in Gurugram (Gurgaon)?","Dr. Kshitij Lochab, who has an Advanced Fellowship in EUS and ERCP, sees patients at Arcura Clinic, Sector 51, Apollo Clinic, Sector 52, and Apollo Hospitals, Golf Course Road. ERCP is done in a hospital with X-ray facilities, after a consultation and review of your reports."]]),

"variceal-banding-evl": dict(
 desc="Variceal banding (EVL) in Gurugram for swollen veins (varices) in liver disease and cirrhosis: why it is done, preparation, sessions, diet after banding and risks. Dr. Kshitij Lochab.",
 intro=["In cirrhosis and some other liver diseases, pressure builds up in the veins that carry blood to the liver. Blood finds other routes, and the veins in the lower food pipe swell. These are called varices. Large varices can burst and cause heavy, dangerous bleeding.",
        "Variceal banding (endoscopic variceal ligation, EVL) places tiny rubber bands around these veins through the scope. The banded vein shrinks, scars and falls off within days. Banding is done to prevent a first bleed in large varices, to stop active bleeding, and to prevent repeat bleeding."],
 prep=["Nothing to eat for 6 to 8 hours before.",
       "Blood tests such as platelet count and clotting are checked.",
       "Tell the doctor about all your medicines, including blood thinners and diuretics.",
       "Come with an adult, as sedation is used."],
 day="You are given a throat spray and sedation. A small cap with bands is fitted to the scope, each vein is gently sucked into the cap and a band is released around it. Several bands may be placed in one session, which takes about 15 to 20 minutes.",
 after=["Mild chest discomfort and pain on swallowing for 1 to 2 days are common.",
        "Take cold liquids for the first few hours, then a soft diet for a day or two, before returning to normal food.",
        "An acid-reducing medicine is sometimes given to help the small banding ulcers heal.",
        "Banding is repeated every 2 to 4 weeks until the varices are gone, followed by check-up endoscopies.",
        "Continue liver medicines as prescribed, and avoid alcohol completely."],
 risks="Chest discomfort and mild pain on swallowing are common and settle quickly. Bleeding from the small ulcer left after a band falls off is uncommon but can be serious, so any sign of bleeding needs urgent care. A narrowing of the food pipe is rare.",
 alarm=["Vomiting blood or black, tarry stools","Dizziness, fainting or a racing heartbeat","Severe chest pain or being unable to swallow"],
 alts="Medicines called beta-blockers lower the pressure in the liver veins and are often used with, or instead of, banding. Varices in the stomach are often treated with glue injection. If bleeding keeps coming back, a TIPS procedure done by a radiologist may be considered.",
 faqs=[["Does banding cure cirrhosis?","No. Banding treats the swollen veins and lowers the risk of bleeding. Treating the cause of liver disease and regular follow-up remain essential."],
       ["Is banding painful?","It is done under sedation. Afterwards, some chest discomfort or pain on swallowing for a day or two is common."],
       ["Who needs banding?","People with cirrhosis who have large varices, those who have bled from varices, and some with high-risk features on endoscopy. Everyone with cirrhosis should have a screening endoscopy to check."],
       ["Where can I get variceal banding in Gurugram?","Dr. Kshitij Lochab sees patients at Arcura Clinic, Sector 51, Apollo Clinic, Sector 52, and Apollo Hospitals, Golf Course Road. In case of vomiting blood, go straight to the nearest emergency department or call 112."]]),

"polyp-removal-emr": dict(
 desc="Polyp removal (polypectomy) and endoscopic mucosal resection (EMR) in Gurugram: why polyps are removed, preparation, recovery, risks and follow-up colonoscopy. Dr. Kshitij Lochab.",
 intro=["Polyps are small growths on the inner lining of the bowel. Most are harmless, but some types (adenomas) can slowly turn into cancer over several years. Removing them during a colonoscopy is one of the most effective ways to prevent colon cancer.",
        "Small polyps are removed with a wire loop (snare) or small forceps. Larger and flat growths are removed by endoscopic mucosal resection (EMR): fluid is injected under the growth to lift it away from the deeper wall, and it is then removed with a snare, sometimes in pieces. Tiny clips may be placed to close the area. EMR is also used for some early flat growths in the stomach and food pipe."],
 prep=["Preparation is the same as for colonoscopy: low-fibre diet, clear liquids the day before, and the full bowel cleansing drink.",
       "Tell the doctor about blood thinners well in advance. They often need to be paused for polyp removal, but only as planned with your doctor.",
       "For large polyps, blood tests may be checked beforehand.",
       "Come with an adult who can take you home."],
 day="The procedure is done under sedation during a colonoscopy. Removing small polyps adds only a few minutes. EMR of a large polyp can take 30 to 60 minutes. You feel no pain from the removal itself, as the bowel lining has no pain nerves.",
 after=["Most people go home the same day. After a large EMR, a short hospital stay may be advised.",
        "Eat light food on the first day.",
        "Avoid heavy lifting and strenuous exercise for a few days after a large EMR.",
        "Restart blood thinners only when your doctor advises.",
        "The tissue report usually takes about a week and decides when your next colonoscopy should be."],
 risks="Bleeding can happen during removal, which is usually stopped at once, or up to about two weeks later, which is uncommon. A tear in the bowel wall is rare and is usually closed with clips through the scope. The risks are higher for large polyps and are explained beforehand.",
 alarm=["More than a few spoonfuls of fresh blood or clots from the back passage","Severe belly pain or a swollen, hard belly","Fever, dizziness or fainting"],
 alts="Surgery to remove a part of the bowel is sometimes needed for very large polyps, or if the tissue report shows cancer that has spread deeper. For most polyps and early flat growths, endoscopic removal avoids an operation.",
 faqs=[["Are all polyps cancer?","No. Most polyps are not cancer. Some types can become cancer over years, which is why they are removed and tested."],
       ["Will removing a polyp hurt?","No. It is done under sedation, and the bowel lining does not feel cutting. Mild bloating afterwards is common."],
       ["Can polyps come back?","New polyps can form, and large polyps removed in pieces can regrow at the same spot. Follow-up colonoscopy on time catches these early."],
       ["Where can I get polyp removal or EMR in Gurugram?","Dr. Kshitij Lochab sees patients at Arcura Clinic, Sector 51, Apollo Clinic, Sector 52, and Apollo Hospitals, Golf Course Road. The procedure is done in a hospital endoscopy unit after a consultation."]]),

"gi-bleeding-treatment": dict(
 desc="GI bleeding treatment by endoscopy in Gurugram: signs of bleeding from the stomach or bowel, emergency care, how bleeding is stopped, recovery and preventing it again. Dr. Kshitij Lochab.",
 intro=["Bleeding from the digestive tract shows up as vomiting blood or coffee-ground material, black and sticky stools, or red blood from the back passage. It can cause weakness, dizziness or fainting, and needs emergency care.",
        "After the patient is stabilised with fluids and, if needed, blood transfusion, an endoscopy is done, usually within 24 hours. The bleeding point is found and treated through the scope with tiny clips, an injection, heat, bands for varices, glue for stomach varices, or a special powder. Common causes are stomach or duodenal ulcers (often from painkillers or H. pylori), varices in liver disease and abnormal blood vessels."],
 prep=["This is usually an emergency procedure. Go to the nearest emergency department or call 112 if you vomit blood or pass black stools.",
       "In hospital, a drip, blood tests and blood transfusion are given as needed, along with medicines through the vein.",
       "Nothing is eaten until the endoscopy is done.",
       "Bring a list of all your medicines, especially painkillers and blood thinners. Do not stop or start any medicine without the treating team's advice."],
 day="The endoscopy is done under sedation, sometimes with an anaesthetist present if the bleeding is heavy. Most bleeding points can be treated in 20 to 45 minutes. Upper bleeding is treated through an upper endoscopy; bleeding from the colon is treated through a colonoscopy after preparation.",
 after=["You stay in hospital, usually for 1 to 3 days, to make sure the bleeding does not return.",
        "Food is restarted gradually once bleeding has stopped.",
        "For ulcers, acid-reducing medicines are given and H. pylori is tested and treated.",
        "Painkillers that cause ulcers are stopped where possible, and blood thinners are restarted when safe.",
        "Iron or other treatment for anaemia is given, and a repeat endoscopy is sometimes planned to check healing."],
 risks="Endoscopy stops most bleeding. Sometimes bleeding returns, and a repeat endoscopy, a radiology procedure (embolisation) or surgery may be needed. Complications of the endoscopy itself, such as a tear, are uncommon.",
 alarm=["Vomiting blood again or passing more black or red stools","Dizziness, fainting, fast heartbeat or breathlessness","Severe belly pain"],
 alts="If endoscopy cannot stop the bleeding, an interventional radiologist can block the bleeding vessel from inside (embolisation), and surgery is the last option. For varices, medicines and TIPS may be used along with endoscopy.",
 faqs=[["Why do painkillers cause bleeding ulcers?","Common painkillers such as NSAIDs reduce the stomach's natural protection, especially in older people and with long use. Ask your doctor before taking them regularly."],
       ["Will I need a blood transfusion?","Only if the blood count is low or bleeding is heavy. Many people do not need one."],
       ["How soon is the endoscopy done?","Usually within 24 hours of arriving in hospital, and sooner if the bleeding is heavy or there are signs of varices."],
       ["Where should I go for GI bleeding in Gurugram?","Go straight to the nearest hospital emergency department or call 112. For follow-up care and preventing another bleed, Dr. Kshitij Lochab sees patients at Arcura Clinic, Sector 51, Apollo Clinic, Sector 52, and Apollo Hospitals, Golf Course Road."]]),

"peg-feeding-tube": dict(
 desc="PEG feeding tube in Gurugram: who needs it, how it is placed by endoscopy, preparation, feeding and tube care at home, and problems to watch for. Dr. Kshitij Lochab.",
 intro=["A PEG (percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy) is a soft feeding tube placed through the skin of the upper belly directly into the stomach, with the help of an endoscope. It provides food, water and medicines when a person cannot swallow safely or enough by mouth for several weeks or longer.",
        "It is more comfortable than a tube through the nose for long-term use, is hidden under clothing, and lowers the chance of food going into the lungs in people with swallowing problems. A PEG can be removed if swallowing recovers."],
 prep=["Nothing to eat or drink through the mouth or any existing nose tube for 6 to 8 hours before.",
       "Blood tests, including clotting, are checked. Blood thinners may need to be paused as planned by the doctor.",
       "An antibiotic is usually given just before the procedure to prevent infection.",
       "The decision is discussed with the patient and family, including how feeding will be managed at home."],
 day="The procedure is done under sedation, with a local anaesthetic at the skin. The endoscope shows the best spot on the stomach, a small cut is made in the skin, and the tube is guided into place and held by a soft disc inside the stomach and a small plate outside. It takes about 20 to 30 minutes.",
 after=["Feeds are usually started within a day, beginning with water and then slowly increasing.",
        "Keep the person sitting up at least halfway during feeds and for about an hour after.",
        "Flush the tube with clean water before and after each feed and medicine to prevent blockage.",
        "Clean the skin around the tube daily and keep it dry. The nurse will show you how to clean and turn the tube once the site has healed.",
        "A dietitian advises on the type and amount of feed."],
 risks="Mild pain at the site for a few days, minor infection, leakage around the tube and blockage are the most common problems and are usually easy to treat. Serious complications such as bleeding or injury to nearby organs are uncommon.",
 alarm=["Pain during feeds, or feed leaking around the tube with redness or pus","Fever, vomiting or a swollen belly","The tube coming out: cover the site and get help within a few hours, as the opening can close quickly"],
 alts="A tube through the nose (nasogastric tube) is used for short periods of a few weeks. A feeding tube can also be placed by a radiologist or surgeon when endoscopy is not possible.",
 faqs=[["Is getting a PEG painful?","It is done under sedation and local anaesthetic. Some soreness at the site for a few days is common and settles with simple painkillers."],
       ["Can medicines be given through the tube?","Yes, most can, as liquids or crushed and mixed with water, but some tablets must not be crushed. Check each medicine with your doctor or pharmacist."],
       ["What should I do if the tube comes out?","Cover the site with a clean dressing and contact the doctor or hospital straight away. The opening starts closing within hours, so prompt replacement is important."],
       ["Where can I get a PEG tube placed in Gurugram?","Dr. Kshitij Lochab sees patients at Arcura Clinic, Sector 51, Apollo Clinic, Sector 52, and Apollo Hospitals, Golf Course Road. PEG is placed in a hospital endoscopy unit after a consultation with the patient and family."]]),

"dilatation-stenting": dict(
 desc="Endoscopic dilatation and stenting in Gurugram for narrowing of the food pipe, achalasia, blocked bile ducts and bowel: how it is done, preparation, eating after, and risks. Dr. Kshitij Lochab.",
 intro=["A narrowing (stricture) in the food pipe, bile duct or bowel can cause food to stick, vomiting, jaundice or blockage. Narrowings can follow acid damage, swallowing chemicals, surgery or radiation, or be caused by a growth.",
        "During an endoscopy, the narrowing can be gently stretched with a balloon or tapered dilators. In achalasia, a condition where the lower food pipe does not relax, a larger balloon is used to stretch the muscle. When a growth is blocking the food pipe, bile duct, duodenum or colon, a stent (a flexible mesh tube) can be placed to keep it open and relieve symptoms."],
 prep=["Nothing to eat for 6 to 8 hours before. For achalasia, liquids only for a day or two before may be advised, so that the food pipe is empty.",
       "Tell the doctor about blood thinners and diabetes medicines.",
       "Bring previous endoscopy, barium and scan reports.",
       "Come with an adult who can take you home."],
 day="The procedure is done under sedation, sometimes with X-ray guidance to position a balloon or stent accurately. It usually takes 20 to 45 minutes. Many narrowings need more than one session, a few weeks apart, to open fully.",
 after=["Start with sips of water after a few hours, then liquids and soft food, before slowly returning to normal food.",
        "Mild chest discomfort for a day is common after dilatation of the food pipe.",
        "With a food pipe stent: eat sitting upright, take small bites, chew very well, and take sips of water during meals. Avoid large pieces of meat and doughy bread.",
        "Plan follow-up as advised, as some narrowings need repeat sessions."],
 risks="Chest discomfort is common for a day. A tear in the wall is uncommon but is the most important risk, especially with achalasia dilatation, and usually shows as severe pain. Stents can sometimes move, block with food or tissue, or cause pain, and may need adjustment or replacement.",
 alarm=["Severe chest, neck or belly pain after the procedure","Fever, breathlessness or vomiting blood","Food sticking again or being unable to swallow saliva"],
 alts="Surgery is an option for some narrowings and for achalasia. Medicines and repeated dilatation are used depending on the cause. The best option depends on the reason for the narrowing, your overall health and your preferences.",
 faqs=[["Will I be able to eat normally after dilatation?","Most people swallow much better after one or more sessions. If the narrowing returns, the dilatation can be repeated."],
       ["What is achalasia?","A condition in which the muscle at the lower end of the food pipe does not relax, so food and liquids collect above it. It causes difficulty swallowing, regurgitation and weight loss, and is treated by stretching or cutting the tight muscle."],
       ["How long does a stent last?","Stents placed for growths are usually left in place for symptom relief. Stents for non-cancer narrowings are often removed after some weeks."],
       ["Where can I get dilatation or stenting in Gurugram?","Dr. Kshitij Lochab sees patients at Arcura Clinic, Sector 51, Apollo Clinic, Sector 52, and Apollo Hospitals, Golf Course Road. The procedure is done in a hospital endoscopy unit after a consultation and review of your reports."]]),
}
